# Custom Strategies

A `ContainerStrategy` owns everything layout-specific for a droppable — drop-zone calculation, sibling translations during drag, and drop/return animations. You pass an instance straight to `<DndDroppable strategy={...} />`, so strategies compose per-container: different containers in the same app can use different strategies without any global registration.

## Built-in strategies

| Factory | Class | Description |
|---------|-------|-------------|
| `sortable(options?)` | `SortableContainerStrategy` | Position-based drop zones with insert previews. Options: `layout: 'vertical' \| 'horizontal' \| 'grid'` (default `'vertical'`), `flow: 'row' \| 'column'` for grid (default `'row'`) |
| `target(options?)` | `TargetContainerStrategy` | Single drop zone covering the whole container, no previews. Good for trash zones, inboxes, unique drop targets |

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { DndDroppable, sortable, target } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';
</script>

<DndDroppable id="list"  strategy={sortable()} />
<DndDroppable id="row"   strategy={sortable({ layout: 'horizontal' })} />
<DndDroppable id="grid"  strategy={sortable({ layout: 'grid' })} />
<DndDroppable id="trash" strategy={target()} />
```

## Writing a custom strategy

Implement the `ContainerStrategy` interface and pass an instance to the droppable. If your strategy needs controller-level state (the shared `DndState` and droppable registry), implement `bindContext()` — it is called once, the first time the droppable mounts.

```ts
import type {
    ContainerStrategy,
    StrategyBindContext,
    DropZone,
    DndMode,
    DndLayout,
    Droppable,
    DragSession,
    AnimationStep,
} from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';
import {
    SortableContainerStrategy,
    GhostToTargetStep,
    GhostReturnStep,
} from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

export class MasonryStrategy implements ContainerStrategy {
    readonly mode: DndMode = 'masonry'
    readonly layout: DndLayout = 'grid'

    private state!: StrategyBindContext['state']
    private droppablesById!: StrategyBindContext['droppablesById']

    bindContext(ctx: StrategyBindContext): void {
        this.state = ctx.state
        this.droppablesById = ctx.droppablesById
    }

    calculateDropZones(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession | null): DropZone[] {
        // your masonry-specific zone calculation
        return []
    }

    getTranslations(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession): Map<string, { x: number; y: number }> {
        return new Map()
    }

    getDropAnimation(session: DragSession, targetZone: DropZone): AnimationStep {
        return new GhostToTargetStep(this.state, targetZone, this.droppablesById)
    }

    getReturnAnimation(session: DragSession): AnimationStep {
        return new GhostReturnStep(this.state, session.originContainerId, session.originPosition, this.droppablesById)
    }
}
```

Use it on the droppable:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { DndDroppable } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';
    import { MasonryStrategy } from './masonry-strategy';

    const masonry = new MasonryStrategy();
</script>

<DndDroppable id="board" strategy={masonry}>
    ...
</DndDroppable>
```

### Extending a built-in strategy

The simplest path: extend `SortableContainerStrategy` and override just the pieces you need. The base class already implements `bindContext`, drop/return animations, and translations for `vertical` / `horizontal` / `grid`.

```ts
import { SortableContainerStrategy, type DropZone, type Droppable, type DragSession } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

export class StickyFirstSortable extends SortableContainerStrategy {
    override calculateDropZones(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession | null): DropZone[] {
        const zones = super.calculateDropZones(droppable, session)
        // remove the zone that would insert before position 0 so the first item can't be displaced
        return zones.filter((z) => z.position !== 0)
    }
}
```

Pass an instance to the droppable the same way: `<DndDroppable strategy={new StickyFirstSortable()} />`.

### Reusing a strategy instance

Strategy instances are stateless across drags — the same instance can be shared between multiple droppables. Instantiate once in your setup and reuse:

```ts
const listStrategy = sortable()

<DndDroppable id="list-a" strategy={listStrategy} />
<DndDroppable id="list-b" strategy={listStrategy} />
```

If you keep per-container state inside the strategy, create a fresh instance per droppable instead.

## Interface

```ts
import type { Droppable, DndState, DragSession, AnimationStep, DropZone, DndMode, DndLayout } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

interface ContainerStrategy {
    readonly mode: DndMode;
    /** Layout hint for strategies with a layout concept (sortable). Target-like strategies may omit it. */
    readonly layout?: DndLayout;

    calculateDropZones(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession | null): DropZone[];
    getTranslations(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession): Map<string, { x: number; y: number }>;
    getDropAnimation(session: DragSession, targetZone: DropZone): AnimationStep;
    getReturnAnimation(session: DragSession): AnimationStep;

    /** Called once when the owning droppable first attaches — receives controller-level state. */
    bindContext?(ctx: StrategyBindContext): void;
    /** Called on every drag start, before any reactive cycle runs. Use it to capture layout snapshots. */
    onSessionStart?(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession): void;
}

interface StrategyBindContext {
    state: DndState;
    droppablesById: Map<string, Droppable>;
}
```

